id: async-initial-values
title: Async Initial Values
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Forms often edit data loaded from an API. A server-state library such as\nTanStack Query should own fetching, caching, loading, and retries; TanStack\nForm should own the editable copy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two useful patterns for async initial values in Angular: resolve the\ndata before constructing the form, or construct the form immediately with\ncomplete fallback values and reset it when data arrives."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"resolve-data-before-constructing-the-editor","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolve data before constructing the editor"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Angular Router resolvers can make the record available synchronously when the\neditor component is constructed. This lets "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" start with the\ncomplete loaded value."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core'\nimport { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router'\nimport type { ResolveFn, Routes } from '@angular/router'\nimport { injectForm } from '@tanstack/angular-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nexport const userResolver: ResolveFn<User> = async (route) => {\n  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${route.paramMap.get('userId')}`)\n  return response.json()\n}\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-user-editor',\n  standalone: true,\n  template: `<form><!-- fields --></form>`,\n})\nexport class UserEditorComponent {\n  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute)\n  private user = this.route.snapshot.data['user'] as User\n\n  form = injectForm({\n    defaultValues: this.user,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n}\n\nexport const routes: Routes = [\n  {\n    path: 'users/:userId',\n    component: UserEditorComponent,\n    resolve: { user: userResolver },\n  },\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This pattern is useful when no form state is needed during loading. It gives\nthe form complete data on its initial construction. If the router reuses the\nsame component instance for another record, reset that form from the new\nresolved data or arrange for a fresh editor instance."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"create-the-form-before-data-arrives","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create the form before data arrives"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can instead use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectForm"},{"type":"text","value":" in the same component as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectQuery"},{"type":"text","value":".\nProvide a static, shape-complete fallback so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" are never\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":", then deliberately reset the untouched form when query data\narrives."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { Component, effect, inject } from '@angular/core'\nimport { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router'\nimport { injectQuery } from '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\nimport { injectForm } from '@tanstack/angular-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nconst emptyUser: User = {\n  firstName: '',\n  lastName: '',\n}\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-user-page',\n  standalone: true,\n  template: `\n    @if (userQuery.isPending()) {\n      <p>Loading…</p>\n    } @else if (userQuery.isError()) {\n      <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n    } @else {\n      <form><!-- fields --></form>\n    }\n  `,\n})\nexport class UserPageComponent {\n  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute)\n  private userId = this.route.snapshot.paramMap.get('userId')!\n\n  userQuery = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['user', this.userId],\n    queryFn: async (): Promise<User> => {\n      const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${this.userId}`)\n      return response.json()\n    },\n  }))\n\n  form = injectForm({\n    defaultValues: emptyUser,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  constructor() {\n    effect(() => {\n      const user = this.userQuery.data()\n      if (user && !this.form.state.isTouched) {\n        this.form.reset(user)\n      }\n    })\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this example, fields remain unmounted during loading, so the user cannot\nedit them before the loaded values arrive. You can render the empty form\nimmediately when that better suits the UI, but then decide how incoming data\nshould interact with edits."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the approach demonstrated by the repository's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"examples/angular/query-integration"},{"type":"text","value":" example."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"background-refetches-and-record-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Background refetches and record changes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultValues"},{"type":"text","value":" establish the form's baseline; they are not a fully controlled\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":" prop. When background data arrives, decide at the product level\nwhether to keep local edits, prompt before replacing them, or call\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.reset(nextValues)"},{"type":"text","value":" deliberately. Do not overwrite in-progress input\nmerely because a query refreshed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When switching to a different record, construct a fresh editor or deliberately\nreset the existing form. Otherwise, values from the previous record can remain\nin the form."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"resolve-data-before-constructing-the-editor","text":"Resolve data before constructing the editor","level":2},{"id":"create-the-form-before-data-arrives","text":"Create the form before data arrives","level":2},{"id":"background-refetches-and-record-changes","text":"Background refetches and record changes","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nForms often edit data loaded from an API. A server-state library such as\nTanStack Query should own fetching, caching, loading, and retries; TanStack\nForm should own the editable copy.\n\nThere are two useful patterns for async initial values in Angular: resolve the\ndata before constructing the form, or construct the form immediately with\ncomplete fallback values and reset it when data arrives.\n\n## Resolve data before constructing the editor\n\nAngular Router resolvers can make the record available synchronously when the\neditor component is constructed. This lets `defaultValues` start with the\ncomplete loaded value.\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, inject } from '@angular/core'\nimport { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router'\nimport type { ResolveFn, Routes } from '@angular/router'\nimport { injectForm } from '@tanstack/angular-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nexport const userResolver: ResolveFn<User> = async (route) => {\n  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${route.paramMap.get('userId')}`)\n  return response.json()\n}\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-user-editor',\n  standalone: true,\n  template: `<form><!-- fields --></form>`,\n})\nexport class UserEditorComponent {\n  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute)\n  private user = this.route.snapshot.data['user'] as User\n\n  form = injectForm({\n    defaultValues: this.user,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n}\n\nexport const routes: Routes = [\n  {\n    path: 'users/:userId',\n    component: UserEditorComponent,\n    resolve: { user: userResolver },\n  },\n]\n```\n\nThis pattern is useful when no form state is needed during loading. It gives\nthe form complete data on its initial construction. If the router reuses the\nsame component instance for another record, reset that form from the new\nresolved data or arrange for a fresh editor instance.\n\n## Create the form before data arrives\n\nYou can instead use `injectForm` in the same component as `injectQuery`.\nProvide a static, shape-complete fallback so `defaultValues` are never\n`undefined`, then deliberately reset the untouched form when query data\narrives.\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, effect, inject } from '@angular/core'\nimport { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router'\nimport { injectQuery } from '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\nimport { injectForm } from '@tanstack/angular-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nconst emptyUser: User = {\n  firstName: '',\n  lastName: '',\n}\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-user-page',\n  standalone: true,\n  template: `\n    @if (userQuery.isPending()) {\n      <p>Loading…</p>\n    } @else if (userQuery.isError()) {\n      <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n    } @else {\n      <form><!-- fields --></form>\n    }\n  `,\n})\nexport class UserPageComponent {\n  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute)\n  private userId = this.route.snapshot.paramMap.get('userId')!\n\n  userQuery = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['user', this.userId],\n    queryFn: async (): Promise<User> => {\n      const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${this.userId}`)\n      return response.json()\n    },\n  }))\n\n  form = injectForm({\n    defaultValues: emptyUser,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  constructor() {\n    effect(() => {\n      const user = this.userQuery.data()\n      if (user && !this.form.state.isTouched) {\n        this.form.reset(user)\n      }\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, fields remain unmounted during loading, so the user cannot\nedit them before the loaded values arrive. You can render the empty form\nimmediately when that better suits the UI, but then decide how incoming data\nshould interact with edits.\n\nThis is the approach demonstrated by the repository's\n`examples/angular/query-integration` example.\n\n## Background refetches and record changes\n\n`defaultValues` establish the form's baseline; they are not a fully controlled\n`values` prop. When background data arrives, decide at the product level\nwhether to keep local edits, prompt before replacing them, or call\n`form.reset(nextValues)` deliberately. Do not overwrite in-progress input\nmerely because a query refreshed.\n\nWhen switching to a different record, construct a fresh editor or deliberately\nreset the existing form. Otherwise, values from the previous record can remain\nin the form.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: async-initial-values\ntitle: Async Initial Values\n---\n\nForms often edit data loaded from an API. A server-state library such as\nTanStack Query should own fetching, caching, loading, and retries; TanStack\nForm should own the editable copy.\n\nThere are two useful patterns for async initial values in Angular: resolve the\ndata before constructing the form, or construct the form immediately with\ncomplete fallback values and reset it when data arrives.\n\n## Resolve data before constructing the editor\n\nAngular Router resolvers can make the record available synchronously when the\neditor component is constructed. This lets `defaultValues` start with the\ncomplete loaded value.\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, inject } from '@angular/core'\nimport { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router'\nimport type { ResolveFn, Routes } from '@angular/router'\nimport { injectForm } from '@tanstack/angular-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nexport const userResolver: ResolveFn<User> = async (route) => {\n  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${route.paramMap.get('userId')}`)\n  return response.json()\n}\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-user-editor',\n  standalone: true,\n  template: `<form><!-- fields --></form>`,\n})\nexport class UserEditorComponent {\n  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute)\n  private user = this.route.snapshot.data['user'] as User\n\n  form = injectForm({\n    defaultValues: this.user,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n}\n\nexport const routes: Routes = [\n  {\n    path: 'users/:userId',\n    component: UserEditorComponent,\n    resolve: { user: userResolver },\n  },\n]\n```\n\nThis pattern is useful when no form state is needed during loading. It gives\nthe form complete data on its initial construction. If the router reuses the\nsame component instance for another record, reset that form from the new\nresolved data or arrange for a fresh editor instance.\n\n## Create the form before data arrives\n\nYou can instead use `injectForm` in the same component as `injectQuery`.\nProvide a static, shape-complete fallback so `defaultValues` are never\n`undefined`, then deliberately reset the untouched form when query data\narrives.\n\n```ts\nimport { Component, effect, inject } from '@angular/core'\nimport { ActivatedRoute } from '@angular/router'\nimport { injectQuery } from '@tanstack/angular-query-experimental'\nimport { injectForm } from '@tanstack/angular-form'\n\ntype User = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n}\n\nconst emptyUser: User = {\n  firstName: '',\n  lastName: '',\n}\n\n@Component({\n  selector: 'app-user-page',\n  standalone: true,\n  template: `\n    @if (userQuery.isPending()) {\n      <p>Loading…</p>\n    } @else if (userQuery.isError()) {\n      <p role=\"alert\">Could not load the user.</p>\n    } @else {\n      <form><!-- fields --></form>\n    }\n  `,\n})\nexport class UserPageComponent {\n  private route = inject(ActivatedRoute)\n  private userId = this.route.snapshot.paramMap.get('userId')!\n\n  userQuery = injectQuery(() => ({\n    queryKey: ['user', this.userId],\n    queryFn: async (): Promise<User> => {\n      const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${this.userId}`)\n      return response.json()\n    },\n  }))\n\n  form = injectForm({\n    defaultValues: emptyUser,\n    onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n      await fetch('/api/users', {\n        method: 'PUT',\n        headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },\n        body: JSON.stringify(value),\n      })\n    },\n  })\n\n  constructor() {\n    effect(() => {\n      const user = this.userQuery.data()\n      if (user && !this.form.state.isTouched) {\n        this.form.reset(user)\n      }\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, fields remain unmounted during loading, so the user cannot\nedit them before the loaded values arrive. You can render the empty form\nimmediately when that better suits the UI, but then decide how incoming data\nshould interact with edits.\n\nThis is the approach demonstrated by the repository's\n`examples/angular/query-integration` example.\n\n## Background refetches and record changes\n\n`defaultValues` establish the form's baseline; they are not a fully controlled\n`values` prop. When background data arrives, decide at the product level\nwhether to keep local edits, prompt before replacing them, or call\n`form.reset(nextValues)` deliberately. Do not overwrite in-progress input\nmerely because a query refreshed.\n\nWhen switching to a different record, construct a fresh editor or deliberately\nreset the existing form. Otherwise, values from the previous record can remain\nin the form.\n"}
